.\" Manual page for ollm, the build manager of the osglecture bundle.
.\" Version and date in the .TH line are updated by "l3build tag".
.TH OLLM 1 "2026-10-15" "ollm v0.15.0" "osglecture bundle"
.SH NAME
ollm \- build manager for osglecture lecture series
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ollm
.RI [ global-options ]
.RB [[ + ] build ]
.RB [[ + ]\c
.IR target " | " \c
.BI \-\-target= target\c
]
.RI [ build-options ]
.RI [ latexmk-options ]
.br
.B ollm
.RI [ global-options ]
.RB [ + ]\c
.BR report " | " check " | " clean " | " prune " | " doctor " | " deploy " | " convertproject
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ollm
.RI [ global-options ]
.RB [ + ] newproject
.B \-\-profiles=taggable\c
.RB | classic
.br
.B ollm standalone
.RI [ options ]
.I file.tex
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ollm
builds the documents of a lecture series written with the
.B osglecture
class.
A series is a project directory with an
.I ollmconfig.toml
manifest and one subdirectory per unit
(for example
.IR 010-introduction ).
From the same unit source,
.B ollm
produces several targets, such as slides, handouts, a presenter version, and
a long-form script, in every configured language.
.PP
Each build runs
.BR latexmk (1)
with LuaLaTeX in an isolated build directory and promotes the successful
result into the project state below
.IR .osglecture .
The promoted state carries cross-unit references, counter continuation, and
the inputs of integration units, so that units can refer to each other
without knowing build paths.
.PP
Without a command word,
.B build
is assumed.
Built-in target names may be given as a bare word or with a historical
.B +
prefix; project-defined targets are selected with
.BR \-\-target .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B build
Build the selected target, language, and scope.
.TP
.B standalone
Build a single document without a series manifest.
Document type and language come from the class options of the document;
.BR \-\-target ,
.BR \-\-language ,
and
.B \-\-all
are not allowed.
.TP
.B report
Describe the discovered units, promoted projections, artifacts, generations,
and dependencies.
Findings do not change the exit status as long as the state is readable.
.TP
.B check
Validate the same data as
.BR report ,
but treat inconsistencies as errors (exit status 3).
Nothing is built; this is the command intended for CI.
.TP
.B clean
Remove build or state data selected by
.B \-\-level
and
.BR \-\-scope .
.TP
.B prune
Remove abandoned temporary states and superseded generations.
.TP
.B doctor
Inspect Perl,
.BR latexmk ,
LuaLaTeX,
.BR kpsewhich ,
the TOML parser, and the required TeX packages; inside a project also the
manifest, definitions, project files, and stored state.
.TP
.B deploy
Copy already promoted PDFs to the targets configured in the manifest.
It does not build and does not create target directories.
.TP
.B newproject
Create
.I ollmconfig.toml
in the current directory, converting a legacy
.I ollmconfig.pl
where present.
An existing TOML manifest is never overwritten.
.TP
.B convertproject
Convert a legacy project configuration where possible.
.SH TARGETS
The bundle defines the following targets:
.TP
.BR slides " (aliases " beamer ", " presentation )
the slides for projection.
.TP
.B handout
a static handout version of the slides.
.TP
.B screen
the presenter version: every page is twice as wide and carries the presenter
page on the right.
.TP
.BR script " (alias " article )
the long-form version.
.PP
Whether a target uses
.BR ltx-talk ,
.BR beamer ,
.BR book ,
or
.B scrbook
is decided by the document profile selected in the manifest, not by the
target.
Projects can register further targets in a definitions directory; see
.I .ollmconfig.local.toml
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-format=text | json
Select human-readable or machine-readable output.
Use
.B json
for CI and tools.
.TP
.BR \-\-color=auto | always | never
Control coloured terminal output.
.TP
.BI \-\-config= file
Use the given project manifest.
.TP
.BI \-\-project\-root= dir
Use the given project root.
.TP
.B \-\-non\-interactive
Never ask questions.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Let
.B deploy
replace a differing target file where the project policy requires an
explicit permission.
.TP
.BR +enforce+ ", " \-\-enforce+
Require the
.B +
prefix on command and built-in target words, so that a file named like a
target is not taken for one.
.B +force+
is a deprecated alias.
.SS Build options
.TP
.BI \-\-target= target
Select a target, including project-registered ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-language= lang\c
.RB ", " lang=\c
.I lang
Select a configured language.
Without it, the project's default language is used if the target supports
it, otherwise the target's first language.
.TP
.BI \-\-source= file
Select the main TeX source (default:
.IR main.tex ).
.TP
.BR \-\-scope=current | unit | series | collection
Select the units the command acts on.
.B current
(the default inside a unit) builds the selected target and language of the
current unit;
.B unit
builds every permitted target and language of the current unit;
.B series
builds the selected target for every applicable unit;
.B collection
builds the integration unit matching the target's document type, from any
directory of the project.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Extend the target and language selection of the chosen scope to every
combination provided by the manifest.
.TP
.B \-\-resolve
Follow the dependencies reported by LaTeX and rebuild stale producers and
consumers up to a fixpoint.
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Force a complete
.B latexmk
rebuild.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the normalized request (or, for
.B clean
and
.BR prune ,
the removal plan) without acting on it.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug=tex | ollm | tex+ollm
Enable diagnostic output for the given layer.
.TP
.BR \-\-warnings=all | important | none
Select which warnings are shown.
.PP
Unknown options starting with a minus sign are passed on to
.B latexmk
when they are compatible with the controlled build; additional RC files and
options that would bypass it are rejected.
.SS Maintenance options
.TP
.BR \-\-level=aux | build | state | all
What
.B clean
removes: auxiliary files (default, keeps the local PDF), the isolated build
directory, the promoted state, or both build and state.
.TP
.B \-\-stale\-units
Let
.B prune
also remove the states of physical units that no longer exist.
.SS Project creation options
.TP
.BR \-\-profiles=taggable | classic
Required for
.BR newproject ,
optional for
.B convertproject
(default there:
.BR classic ).
.B taggable
uses
.B ltx-talk
and
.B book
with Tagged PDF and creates
.IR Include/documentmetadata.tex ;
.B classic
uses
.B beamer
and
.B scrbook
without tagging.
Single targets can be changed later with
.B profile
in the manifest.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ollmconfig.toml
The project manifest in the project root.
.B ollm
finds it from any unit directory.
.TP
.I .ollmconfig.local.toml
Optional, usually unversioned additional search paths for target and
profile definitions.
.TP
.I Include/projectconfig.tex
Project-wide TeX configuration read by the
.B osglecture
class.
.TP
.I .osglecture/
Build directories and promoted state.
If no
.B ollm
or
.B latexmk
process is running, the whole directory may be deleted as a last-resort
repair; units then have to be built directly again.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ollm/config.toml ", " ~/.config/ollm/config.toml
Personal configuration (preferred bundle preset) on Unix.
.TP
.I %APPDATA%\(rsollm\(rsconfig.toml
Personal configuration on Windows.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OLLM_USER_CONFIG
Path of the personal configuration file; overrides the default locations.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the personal configuration on Unix.
.TP
.B APPDATA
Base directory of the personal configuration on Windows.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or a readable report.
.TP
.B 1
Build, deployment, or state error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid invocation or configuration.
.TP
.B 3
.B check
found an inconsistency.
.TP
.B 69
A required program or execution feature is unavailable.
.TP
.B 70
Internal error.
.TP
.B 128+\c
.I signal
Termination by a signal on POSIX systems, in particular 130 for Ctrl-C.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the German slides of the unit in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ollm slides \-\-language=de
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a project-registered target in English:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ollm build \-\-target=solutions \-\-language=en
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build the script of every unit, then the collected script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ollm script \-\-scope=series
ollm script \-\-scope=collection
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate the project state in CI:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ollm check \-\-format=json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start a new tagged project:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ollm newproject \-\-profiles=taggable
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR latexmk (1),
.BR lualatex (1)
.PP
The full user manual is available as
.I ollm-en.pdf
and
.I ollm-de.pdf
(run
.B texdoc ollm\-en
or
.BR "texdoc ollm\-de" ).
